WEBVTT

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:05.810
So far on today's show, we've learned how data collected by the hurricane hunters helps

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.260
meteorologists at the Weather Channel predict hurricanes.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:10.800
But what about NASA?

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.760
What does NASA have to do with hurricanes?

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.310
I thought you'd never ask.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:21.060
Without NASA, or the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, we wouldn't be able

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.340
to see Earth from space.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:28.410
NASA's bird's-eye view of our planet has revolutionized observations of the Earth's

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:29.800
atmosphere, continents,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.800
A few years back, NASA launched geostationary satellites that monitor the weather above the Earth.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:43.170
Geostationary means that the satellite orbits the Earth at about the same speed the Earth rotates.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.700
The satellite collects weather data as it hovers over the same point on the Earth.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.800
The data is then sent back to Earth for our use.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.940
Thanks to NASA, today there is a worldwide network of satellites above our Earth,

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.910
Collecting weather data over the whole world and transferring these data

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.380
back to us on the ground.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:04.290
But NASA's always looking towards the future,

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:07.030
towards developing new and better technologies

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.330
for observing and predicting severe weather,

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:11.330
like hurricanes and tornadoes.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.920
We're here at the NASA Langley Research Center

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:14.920
in Hampton, Virginia,

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.260
to meet a scientist who's developing a gift

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:18.860
for future meteorologists.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.760
Our gifts provide a more complete picture

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:27.740
of the Earth's atmosphere.

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:29.970
How does remote sensing work?

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.200
What does the information collected about the water vapor tell us about a storm?

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:40.180
NASA Langley Research Center is developing a new technology which will allow meteorologists

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.420
to better predict how strong a hurricane will be and where it will cover shore.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:48.850
Scientists like me are working with professors and students at the University of Wisconsin

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:54.470
and at the Utah State University on the Geostationary Imaging Fourier Transform Spectrometer.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.960
Because acronyms are easier to say, we call it the GIFS.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.840
GIFS is actually an instrument.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.580
that will be flown aboard a geostationary satellite

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.080
about 22,000 miles above the Earth.

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.490
We believe our new technology will be a gift to mankind

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:12.120
and that it will enable people to avoid the loss of their property

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.320
and even possibly their lives

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.660
by warning them of approaching hazardous weather,

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.660
such as tornadoes and hurricanes.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.380
Okay, but how will GIFs improve weather predictions?

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.560
Well, GIFs will provide meteorologists

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.740
with a complete picture of our Earth's atmosphere.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:29.890
Currently, upper-air observations like temperature, water vapor, and wind

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:35.080
are obtained by launching weather balloons every day twice a day these balloons rise

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:40.220
through the atmosphere and collect weather data over land areas however the balloons are spaced

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:46.210
very far apart and give us only spotty data gifts will collect the same data from space every 10

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:53.690
seconds over land as well as over the sea where hurricanes come from wow every 10 seconds dr

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:59.790
smith how will it do that using new infrared digital camera technology gifts will have several thousand

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.820
more sensors in current satellites to measure atmospheric temperature, water vapor, and wind.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:09.780
Let's take water vapor for instance. Water vapor is actually water in its gaseous state,

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:15.250
so it's invisible to the human eye. When water vapor molecules become liquid water or ice crystals,

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:20.850
a cloud forms. Current satellites measure water in the form of clouds, but they do not measure very well

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:23.290
the water vapor that causes the clouds to form.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:29.880
Being able to sense the water vapor before clouds form will allow us to predict how, when, and where

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.030
storms will form and where they will go. That's great. Sounds like GIFs will provide

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:39.730
meteorologists with information on hurricanes even before they form. This can only help

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:44.390
save more lives because the more forecasters know, the quicker they can warn people on the

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:50.290
coast. But you keep saying that GIFs will sense water vapor. What do you mean by sense?

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:55.050
Oh, good question. GIFs measures elements of the atmosphere like temperature and water

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:59.660
vapor without touching them. It's called remote sensing and our eyes do it every day.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.660
Your eyes can sense or measure how a person is feeling without touching them.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.580
All you need to do is look at them, and you'll be able to tell how they are feeling.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.620
Oh, okay. I understand how people sense things, but GIFS is an instrument.

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:13.630
How does it sense water vapor?

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.400
By using an infrared camera. Let me show you.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:20.340
This picture of a man in glasses was taken with an infrared camera,

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:24.000
seeing through the air and sensing only hard, solid objects,

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.760
like the wall, his face, and his glasses.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.960
Here we see the brightness of the man's face and the...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.080
darkness of his glasses.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:36.790
This means his face is giving off heat and is relatively warm, whereas his glasses on

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:40.540
the wall are not giving off as much heat and are therefore relatively cold.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.760
This picture is a good example of what current satellites can see from space.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.840
The second picture was taken with an infrared camera, seeing air molecules, as well as

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.060
hard, solid objects.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.320
Here we can see his breath exhaled from his mouth.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:59.430
This is because the infrared camera senses that his breath, which contains water vapor,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.940
warm relative to the cold wall behind it. The second picture is a good example of how

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:07.060
GIF's technology will improve weather detection.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:11.800
You said the water vapor in his breath is warm, so I guess that means it gives off heat.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.660
Yes, both water vapor and clouds give off or radiate heat. GIFs can't feel the heat,

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:22.490
but being an infrared camera, it can sense the amount of heat the water vapor radiates.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:28.560
An analogy is when you close your eyes on a partly cloudy day. When the sun comes out from behind

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:29.980
the clouds you don't have to

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.590
If you open your eyes to know the sun is there, you can sense or feel the heat of the sun radiating on your face.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:38.680
Speaking of heat, remember the graph Valerie showed you?

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:44.430
Heat, but specifically temperature, is an important variable when describing what is going on in a hurricane.

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:48.250
GIFs will measure the heat from water vapor even before clouds form.

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:52.810
This will allow us to predict the formation and intensity of upcoming storms,

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:55.940
like how much rain will fall or how severe the winds will be.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:58.920
Arcanes are known for their wins.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:05.250
sense winds too? Absolutely. Dr. Lyons told us earlier that the steering winds

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:09.540
determine where a hurricane is going. There are very few clouds away from the

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:13.150
storm where the steering winds are. GIFS measures the movement of the water

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:17.620
vapor at all altitudes. These motions are the steering winds which determine the

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:21.840
speed and the direction of the hurricane. Well Dr. Smith, GIFS really is a

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.560
gift for forecasters and for people who are affected by severe storms like

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.980
hurricanes. That's right. In fact, NASA Goddard Space Flight Center in Green

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.840
Three Bell, Maryland, is working with us to integrate GIFS technology on future weather

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:34.280
satellites.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:39.880
Visit this website to learn more about GIFS technology and NASA's dedication to help

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:41.760
science better understand our planet.

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:42.990
Thanks, Dr. Smith.

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:48.020
You know, future NASA technology, like GIFS, will provide us with more accurate satellite

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:48.540
measurements.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:53.480
By combining GIFS technology with the data from the hurricane hunters, meteorologists

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:57.640
like Dr. Lyons will be better able to predict hurricanes and help save lives.
